      execution_speed: [16, 0.74, "d", "D"],
      // [src_kubrick_digiulio, src_kubrick_zeiss_harlan, src_kubrick_harlan_ss, src_kubrick_ringer_ai | 1972-75; 1970-99; 1977-99 | firsthand supplier + producer + secondary] When Zeiss said the f/0.7 lens could not be used on a motion-picture camera, Kubrick was 'not ready to take a no' (Harlan) and, in DiGiulio's words, 'persisted' until DiGiulio agreed to try; two independent witnesses describe the same episode. Harlan also says he returned to Traumnovelle after its 1970 Warner contract until it became Eyes Wide Shut, and The Ringer traces the A.I. story from 1977 through writers cycled to 1995 and the 1993 announcement. Some projects were also dropped (Harlan), so the score is not extreme.
      persistence: [78, 0.72, "d", "A"],
      // [src_kubrick_digiulio, src_kubrick_ciment_colleagues, src_kubrick_harris_filmcomment, src_kubrick_filmstories_spartacus | 1960; 1971-80 | firsthand supplier + colleagues + secondary] DiGiulio (1976) says he liked to own all his equipment, even building his own location vehicle, and insisted a 20-to-1 zoom be built for him rather than rented; Senior (1980) says he supervised everything, including refusing a set stills photographer despite objections from Time and Newsweek; in Harris's account he could not continue with Brando 'dictating every step' on a Paramount western. After Spartacus he remarked that decisions may not be respected unless the contract says so (his own words, via Film Stories).
