      // [src_kubrick_harris_filmcomment, src_kubrick_ciment_colleagues, src_kubrick_trumbull | c.1962-63; 1973-75; 1965-66 | firsthand collaborators] In Harris's account Kubrick told him he and Terry Southern thought Red Alert would work better as satire, a turn Harris then thought would 'blow his whole career'. Adam (undated interview) says Kubrick was set against studio sets for Barry Lyndon and preferred painters such as Gainsborough and Hogarth as visual reference, against Adam's own conception, and Trumbull says he chose Bonestell-style peaks over Trumbull's lunar-photograph moon model. Dual_edged: Adam says the location shooting made the film very expensive.
