      resourcefulness: [78, 0.72, "d", "A"],
      // [src_kubrick_bfi_clarke, src_kubrick_brown_steadicam, src_kubrick_harris_filmcomment, src_kubrick_ciment_colleagues | 1955; 1964; 1971; 1974 | archive letter + firsthand collaborators] By late March 1964 he wrote to Arthur C. Clarke proposing a science-fiction collaboration with three areas of interest (BFI, letter is his own words); in 1974 he answered a Steadicam demonstration film by telex and later engaged Brown for The Shining (Brown). Harris says Kubrick asked for his help selling Killer's Kiss to TV, which led to their partnership, and Senior says Kubrick phoned him in November 1971 to work on the A Clockwork Orange release.
      proactive_agency: [84, 0.8, "d", "A"],
      // [src_kubrick_2001edits, src_kubrick_harlan_bfi, src_kubrick_ciment_colleagues, src_kubrick_brown_steadicam | 1968; 1980; 1973-75 | secondary + producer + firsthand colleagues] After the April 1968 premiere he re-cut 2001 with Ray Lovejoy on 5-9 April, cutting about 19 minutes (T. E. Brown, quoting him), and Harlan says he agreed to shorten The Shining after its reception ('He was not that stubborn'); Adam says Kubrick adopted his idea of an older house for Lady Lyndon, and Brown says Kubrick later admitted picking takes for performance rather than technical differences. Against this, Adam says he could not talk Kubrick out of location shooting, so the score is a moderate lean.
